Hungarian law contains no offence of hitchhiking (''stoppolás''), which is legal on ordinary roads; what it defines precisely is where a pedestrian may not be. [https://njt.hu/jogszabaly/1975-1-20-24.25 § 21(9) of the KRESZ (Decree 1/1975. (II. 5.) KPM–BM)] forbids pedestrians to walk along or cross an ''autópálya'' — "''a leállósávon is''", the hard shoulder included — and, for an ''autóút'', bans walking on the hard shoulder ''and the verge'' too and crossing anywhere but a junction. So neither motorway class leaves a hitchhiker anywhere lawful to stand, slip roads included. Use the last roundabout or petrol station before the motorway, or ask drivers at a service area, where they have stopped anyway. On ''főútvonalak'' and lesser roads, § 21(1) puts pedestrians on the hard shoulder or verge and thumbing there breaks no rule.
